e a mirror that shows another room, and walk away clean. Nina Kowalski considered it for exactly as long as it took to be afraid — then refused.\n\nThe nursery door, locked for decades, opened for Nina Kowalski like it had been waiting. Inside: dust, a small bed, and a mirror that shows another room's twin — the other half of whatever had been broken that terrible night."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Night', 'text': "What followed wasn't a fight; it was a siege of the soul. the drowned choir wore the faces of everyone Nina Kowalski loved, starting with the old caretaker Gus, begging to be let in, to be believed, to be obeyed. Nina Kowalski held the line with the oldest weapon: names, spoken true.\n\nNina Kowalski understood then what had to be done: not destroy a mirror that shows another room, but complete it. Join the halves. Speak the name. Give the drowned choir what it had wanted all along — to be seen, to be mourned, to be released.\n\nIt came on the anniversary, as Nina Kowalski knew it would. The fog pressed against the windows of the marsh village like a face. a mirror that shows another room activated — played, filled, showed, rang, drew, unlocked — and the drowned choir stepped out of the walls."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Aftermath', 'text': "Madame Zara sent a note: 'You did what I couldn't. The house is clean.' Nina Kowalski framed it. Not from pride — from proof. Proof that the dark can be answered.\n\nthe marsh village is for sale again, and Nina Kowalski is staying. Someone has to be here who knows. Someone has to keep the light on. That someone is Nina Kowalski now.\n\nDawn in the marsh village was just dawn — fog burning off, birds being birds. Nina Kowalski slept fourteen hours and woke to the old caretaker Gus making pancakes like nothing had happened, which was exactly the right thing."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Epilogue: The Light Kept', 'text': "the old caretaker Gus visits every anniversary with pancakes and bad jokes. They don't talk about that night. They don't need to. It's in the walls — but the walls, now, are just walls.\n\nfear is a liar, face it — Nina Kowalski wrote it on the first page of the archive, and on the last. Whoever comes next will read it. Whoever comes next will know.\n\nSometimes a mirror that shows another room catches the light oddly, and Nina Kowalski feels the old chill. Then Nina Kowalski touches it, whole and quiet, and remembers the choice: to face it, to mend it, to stay."}]}

